```python
def update(node, lo, hi, idx, val):
    if lo == hi:
        tree[node] = val
        return
    mid = (lo + hi) // 2
    if idx <= mid:
        update(2*node, lo, mid, idx, val)
    else:
        update(2*node+1, mid+1, hi, idx, val)
    tree[node] = tree[2*node] + tree[2*node+1]  # recombine after the recursive call returns
```

Only one root-to-leaf path is touched — exactly the nodes whose range contains `idx` — so a single point update is O(log n), touching a logarithmic number of ancestors rather than rebuilding anything. The recombination step after each recursive call is what keeps every ancestor's cached aggregate correct after the leaf changes.
